The midterm proposal and final examination are two stages of the *same final project*.

The midterm asks:

#quote(block: true)[*Can you formulate a meaningful condensed matter physics problem and develop a reasonable strategy for answering it?*]The final examination asks:

#quote(block: true)[*After investigating the problem, can you explain the physics clearly, defend your reasoning, and connect it to the broader ideas of condensed matter physics?*]#line(length: 100%, stroke: gray)

=== 3. Relationship Between the Midterm and Final Examination <id-3-relationship-between-the-midterm-and-final-examination>

At the midterm stage, you should establish

$ boxed "Question"+ "Model"+ "Preliminary Understanding"+ "Plan" $
By the final examination, this should develop into

$ boxed "Question"arrow.r "Model"arrow.r "Derivation \/ Evidence"arrow.r "Physical Understanding" $
The most important development between the two stages is therefore *not simply adding more references, calculations, or slides*.

It is turning your proposed question into an argument that you genuinely understand.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: gray)

