==== 4.1 Final Presentation — 15 minutes <id-4-1-final-presentation-15-minutes>

Your final presentation should develop the proposal into a coherent physical argument:

$ boxed "Question"arrow.r "Minimal Model"arrow.r "Derivation \/ Evidence"arrow.r "Result"arrow.r "Physical Interpretation" $
The goal is not to present everything you learned about the topic.

Instead, your presentation should answer a specific physical question.

By the end of the presentation, your audience should understand:

+ what you wanted to understand\;
+ why the question was nontrivial\;
+ what minimal model you used\;
+ what calculation or argument produced the key result\;
+ what the result means physically.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: gray)

==== 4.2 Project Oral Examination — 10 minutes <id-4-2-project-oral-examination-10-minutes>

After the presentation, you will answer questions related to your project.

These questions may ask you to:

- explain one of your equations physically\;
- reproduce or reconstruct a simple argument\;
- justify an assumption\;
- predict what happens when a parameter changes\;
- distinguish essential ingredients from unnecessary ones\;
- connect your project to another concept from the course\;
- reason through a situation that was not explicitly included in your presentation.

This is why each project topic contains an *"If You Really Understand This..."* question.

You should eventually be able to answer that question without relying on your slides, notes, the internet, or AI.

The goal is not memorization.

The goal is to determine whether you can *reason with the physics of your project*.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: gray)

==== 4.3 General Condensed Matter Physics Oral Examination — 15 minutes <id-4-3-general-condensed-matter-physics-oral-examination-15-minutes>

The final 15 minutes will contain broader questions from Condensed Matter I.

These questions are *not restricted to your final-project topic*.

The emphasis will be on physical reasoning and connections among the major concepts of the course.

You may encounter questions involving:

- second quantization\;
- tight-binding models\;
- Bloch theorem\;
- band structures\;
- Fermi surfaces\;
- Berry phase\;
- topological bands\;
- interacting fermions\;
- response functions\;
- Fermi-liquid theory\;
- symmetry breaking\;
- collective modes\;
- renormalization group\;
- path integrals\;
- Green's functions\;
- spectral representations\;
- diagrammatics\;
- linear response\;
- quantum entanglement\;
- tensor networks\;
- topological order\;
- emergent gauge theories\;
- ETH and quantum thermalization\;
- many-body localization.

You should expect follow-up questions such as:

#quote(block: true)[*Why?*]#quote(block: true)[*Can you show that?*]#quote(block: true)[*What assumption did you use?*]#quote(block: true)[*What happens if I change this parameter?*]#quote(block: true)[*What breaks if I remove this assumption?*]#quote(block: true)[*Can you give me the simplest example?*]The objective is to evaluate whether you can *think using condensed matter physics*, rather than only reproduce prepared material.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: gray)

=== 5. The Overall Logic of the Project <id-5-the-overall-logic-of-the-project>

The project is designed to develop in stages over the semester.

===== Homework — Build the Tools <homework-build-the-tools>

Through the homework assignments, you learn how to work with the central theoretical tools and concepts of condensed matter physics.

===== Midterm — Formulate the Problem <midterm-formulate-the-problem>

For the *25% written proposal*, demonstrate:

$ boxed "I know what I want to understand and how I plan to investigate it." $
===== Final — Explain and Defend the Physics <final-explain-and-defend-the-physics>

For the *30% final examination*, demonstrate:

$ boxed "I now understand the physics well enough to explain it and reason with it." $
The overall progression of the course is therefore:

$ boxed "Learn the tools"arrow.r "Formulate a problem"arrow.r "Investigate it"arrow.r "Explain it"arrow.r "Defend your understanding" $
This progression is more important than simply obtaining a correct final answer.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: gray)

=== 6. Final Perspective <id-6-final-perspective>

The final project is not intended to test how much information you can collect about a topic.

It is intended to test whether you can take a condensed matter physics problem and progressively make it your own.

At the midterm, you should be able to say:

#quote(block: true)[*I know what question I am asking, why it is interesting, what model I should use, and what I still need to figure out.*]By the final examination, you should be able to say:

#quote(block: true)[*I can now explain what happens, why it happens, what assumptions are required, and how this example connects to the broader principles of condensed matter physics.*]That transition—from *having a question* to *owning the physical reasoning behind the answer*—is the central goal of the project.